FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my %c;
    @c{qw(b a c)}++;
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($c{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %c), "\n";   # perl a=u b=u c=1   PCL (empty line)
    my @a = (1, 1, 1);
    @a[0, 2]++;
    print "@a\n";                                                           # perl 1 1 2         PCL 1 1 1
    my %e;
    @e{qw(p q)} += 5;
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($e{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %e), "\n";   # perl p=u q=5       PCL (empty line)
    my %f;
    ++@f{qw(m n)};
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($f{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %f), "\n";   # perl m=u n=1       PCL (empty line)

perl: the slice is an lvalue (every element is vivified, as for any lvalue slice) and the scalar operator applies to its LAST element.  The everyday idiom is `@seen{@list}++` / `@count{LIST}++` to make a set of keys (the values do not matter, `keys` does): PCL leaves the hash EMPTY, silently.

WHERE (emitted Lisp): `(p-post++ (p-hslice %c @k))` -- the increment is applied to the rvalue slice's result, so neither the vivification nor the write reaches the hash.  Expected: vivify every element of the slice as an lvalue, then modify the last element's place.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): the program becomes identical to perl when the slice increment is spelled `$c{$_}++ for @$_` (the per-key loop), so it is the only cause.

THE PROGRAM: 0243-Common-sorted-list collects the union of several lists as hash keys with a slice increment and prints the sorted keys: PCL prints nothing.
